  "response": "I checked the scan against the fixture source. The four `swallowed_error` findings fall into two groups. Three are build steps that catch a fatal error and return normally. The fourth is a parser that turns a broken config file into `{}`. None of the four is a genuinely recoverable error.\n\nOne correction to the scanner first. All three `log_without_error` findings say the error value is never passed on, and the source contradicts that. Each handler calls `handleError(error)` (`src/errors.ts:16`), which prints the stack and sets `process.exitCode = 1`. So the stack is not lost. The part of the claim that holds is \"no rethrow or rejected promise\": the function resolves as if it succeeded. The only failure signal is the global `exitCode`, and `build()` callers never see it. That includes the test suite and programmatic API users. Other tasks keep running too: `mainTasks` and its `onSuccess` hook run even when type declarations failed.\n\n## The findings\n\n**1. `crime_00021` — `src/api-extractor.ts:197`, `runDtsRollup`: not recoverable. Discarding it hides the failure.**\n- `\"protected operation: const start = Date.now() (+6 more statement(s))\"`\n- `\"calls inside the protected region: Date.now, Math.floor, logger.info, cleanDtsFiles, rollupDtsFiles, logger.success\"`\n- `\"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"`\n- `\"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; a log call is present but the error value is not passed to it; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"`\n\nIf `rollupDtsFiles` fails, the `.d.ts` output is missing or partial. That's a broken release, not a fallback. This function also catches tsc's failures: `runTypeScriptCompiler` returns `undefined` on error, and this function then throws `'Unexpected internal error: dts exports is not define'` into its own swallowing catch. The scan shows `test_gap: 0.75`, so no test covers this path.\n\n**2. `crime_00019` — `src/tsc.ts:215`, `runTypeScriptCompiler`: not recoverable. Discarding it hides the failure.**\n- `\"protected operation: const start = Date.now() (+6 more statement(s))\"`\n- `\"calls inside the protected region: Date.now, Math.floor, logger.info, emit, logger.success, getDuration\"`\n- `\"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"`\n- `\"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; a log call is present but the error value is not passed to it; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"`\n\nA type error in `emit` means declarations can't be produced. Returning `undefined` in place of `exports` passes the problem on to `runDtsRollup` as a misleading \"internal error\".\n\n**3. `crime_00027` — `src/rollup.ts:187`, `runRollup`: not recoverable, but here the signal does get through.**\n- `\"protected operation: const start = Date.now() (+7 more statement(s))\"`\n- `\"calls inside the protected region: Date.now, Math.floor, logger.info, rollup, Promise.all, options.outputConfig.map\"`\n- `\"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"`\n- `\"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; a log call is present but the error value is not passed to it; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"`\n\nThis code runs in a worker thread, where `handleError` calls `parentPort.postMessage('error')`. The parent (`src/index.ts:240`) rejects on that message. So this one is fragile but not silent. Two problems remain. The `catch { parentPort?.postMessage('error') }` in `startRollup` (`src/rollup.ts:224`) can never run, because `runRollup` never throws. And `'success'` is still posted after a failure, so the result depends on message order. It should rethrow, but it's not what turned CI green.\n\n**4. `crime_00034` — `src/utils.ts:135`, `jsoncParse`: claimed to be recoverable, but for its actual callers it isn't.**\n- `\"protected operation: return new Function(\\`return ${strip(data).trim()}\\`)()\"`\n- `\"what happens to the failure: the handler returns \\`{}\\` without inspecting the error\"`\n- `\"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; no logging, telemetry, or error-wrapping call; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"`\n- `\"the catch declares no binding, so the error object is not even available to the handler\"`\n\nThe code comment says this matches tsc's behaviour. But the only caller is `loadJson` in `src/load.ts`, which loads tsup's own JSON config, and `loadJson` wraps the call in a `try/catch` that throws `Failed to parse ...`. That handler can never fire. A malformed config gives a successful build with default settings. That's a silent misconfiguration rather than a failed build, so it comes after the dts sites.\n\n## Fix first: `runDtsRollup` (`src/api-extractor.ts:197`)\n\nThis one fix closes the whole `experimentalDts` path. Its own failures reject, and tsc failures reject too, because the `!exports` guard throws into this catch. Replacement:\n\n```ts\n  } catch (error) {\n    logger.error('dts', 'Build error')\n    throw error\n  }\n```\n\n`handleError` is dropped on purpose. With the rethrow, the rejection travels `experimentalDtsTask` → `dtsTask` → `build()` → `main().catch(handleError)` in `src/cli-default.ts:5`. The top-level handler prints the stack and sets the exit code once, so keeping `handleError` here would print the error twice. Programmatic `build()` callers also get a rejected promise now, which is the signal CI has been missing.\n\nNext, apply the same change to `runTypeScriptCompiler`, so the real tsc error is what propagates instead of the \"exports is not define\" stand-in. After that, give `runRollup` the same treatment and make `jsoncParse` throw.\n\nI haven't edited or run anything. One thing to check before merging: with the rethrow, an `experimentalDts` failure during watch mode will reject instead of only logging.",
